nd The Economist, lean toward Brown. Polling since mid-September shows Brown narrowly ahead, with even Republican-leaning pollsters finding him up two to five points.\n\nBrown has raised far more money than Husted, but outside groups have put $11.4 million behind attacks on him, and Trump is campaigning for Husted in the final stretch. Markets give Brown roughly a 57 to 62 percent chance. The race remains genuinely competitive, with the environment and late spending likely to decide it.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7221, "margin": 2.94}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7246, "margin": 3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.6986, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.7024, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.6349, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.6393, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.6851, "margin": 2.5}]}